# top — real-world examples

## Basic usage (Linux / procps-ng)

```sh
# Start top with default view
top

# Update every 5 seconds instead of the default 3
top -d 5

# Run 3 iterations then exit (good for logging)
top -n 3

# Batch mode — plain text output, pipe-friendly
top -b -n 1 > /tmp/top-snapshot.txt
```

## Filter what you see

```sh
# Show only david's processes on the homelab box
top -u david

# Watch specific PIDs (e.g. Plex Media Server)
top -p $(pgrep -d, -f 'Plex Media Server')

# Sort by memory instead of CPU (interactive: press Shift+M)
top -o %MEM
```

## Homelab monitoring

```sh
# Snapshot the top CPU consumers on mediaserver over ssh
ssh david@mediaserver 'top -b -n 1 | head -22'

# Log a snapshot every minute for an hour (troubleshooting a runaway process)
for i in $(seq 60); do top -b -n 1 | head -20 >> /tmp/top.log; sleep 60; done
```

## Useful interactive keys

```text
h        help
M        sort by memory
P        sort by CPU (default)
1        show each CPU core separately
k        kill a process (prompts for PID)
u        filter by user
c        toggle full command line
W        save current config (writes toprc)
q        quit
```

## macOS / BSD variant flags (original examples)

```sh
# Update framework stats every <interval> samples
top -i <interval>

# Set the delay between updates to <delay> seconds
top -s <delay>

# Event counting: accumulative / delta / absolute mode
top -a
top -d
top -e

# Skip (-F) or calculate (-f, default) shared library statistics
top -F
top -f

# Order the display by <key> in descending order
top -o <key>

# Print usage and exit
top -h
```

## Flag reference (Linux procps-ng)

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-b` | batch mode (non-interactive, for scripts/logs) |
| `-n N` | exit after N iterations |
| `-d SEC` | delay between updates |
| `-u USER` | show only USER's processes |
| `-p PID` | monitor specific PID(s) |
| `-o FIELD` | sort by field (e.g. %MEM, %CPU) |
| `-H` | show individual threads |
| `-c` | show full command line |
